\title{Fixing a terminological mistake: \em{triangulatedness}}
\date{2026-11-26}
\author{samueltoth}

\p{This entry is regarding a relatively small change to the library that I
nonetheless wanted to highlight. The changelog has been quiet lately as I
recently started my PhD studies at [UoN](unott) under
the supervision of [Nicolai Kraus](nicolaikraus); and [Fredrik](fredrikbakke)
has been busy writing up his thesis. I have been working a little in the
background on some boring, but much needed refactorings and cleanups of the
library, and [Fredrik Bakke](fredrikbakke) and [Mark Williams](markwilliams)
have been working on some more exciting formalisations, which will hopefully be
merged soon.}

\p{The change in question relates to notion of [square complete](stt-00LD) types.
These form a [reflective subuniverse](stt-00IF) consisting of types local to the
[triangulation of the square](stt-00CC), which is the embedding of a pair of
triangles glued along their long edge into the square. This condition is
an aparent weakening of [simpliciality](stt-00AC), although [a recent
note](SGWB) of [Schellingerhout](robschellingerhout), [Gratzer](danielgratzer),
[Weinberger](jonathanweinberger), and [Buchholtz](ulrikbuchholtz) shows that
for crisp types, they are equivalent.}

\p{We had previously used the terminology \em{triangulated} for this notion,
but this is a bad choice for a number of reasons.  Because there is an inclusion of
the walking triangulated square into the square, every square in a type
naturally gives rise to a triangulated square in that type by restriction. Being
\em{square complete} gives you an inverse to this map, and so a square complete
type should be thought of as being a type where one is able to glue together a
pair of compatible triangles into a square. Naturally, the triangulated square
is not square complete (if it were then every type would be); this fact sounds
quite non-sensical with our previous choice of terminology, and this gets even
worse when you take \em{"triangulation"} to be the name of the modal operator:
"the triangulation of the triangulated square is the square".}

\p{I apologise for any confusion to which this naming choice may have contributed,
but I am happy that we have settled on what I think is a much clearer and more
intuitive convention.}
